      "history_summaries": [
        "**Round 1: Fix output_correct_on_done (DUT bug)**. Root cause: when inputs are equal or zero, `done` fires the cycle after `load`, but `oReg` was never updated during the load phase, causing assertion failure because `oReg` still holds 0. Fix: added `oReg := Mux(io.a < io.b, io.a, io.b)` inside the `when(load)` block (line 44 in original, expanded at line 46 in fixed). This ensures oReg holds the correct GCD result when done fires immediately upon the next clock edge. For multi-cycle cases, the `.elsewhen(done)` block still overwrites oReg with the correct value. Fixed file: `gcd.scala`. Assertion `output_correct_on_done` preserved as-is.",
        "Round 2: Fix output_correct_on_done for multi-cycle computation case (DUT bug). Root cause: Round 1 fixed the trivial case (load-then-done) but for multi-cycle GCD computations, oReg retained the stale min(io.a, io.b) value from the load phase. When done fires after several iterations, the `.elsewhen(done)` block assigns oReg but the register update takes effect one cycle later — one cycle too late for the combinational assertion. Fix: added `oReg := Mux(x < y, x, y)` inside the `.elsewhen(busyReg && !done)` computation block (line ~100), so oReg tracks the running minimum on every computation cycle. By the time `done` fires, oReg already holds the correct GCD result. The assertion `output_correct_on_done` label is preserved exactly. No homologous assertions found (single unique assertion of this form)."
